{%- comment -%}
  PG Wordmark: kern the T in POCKET ERA on its own, without touching the rest.

  THE PROBLEM WITH THE PREVIOUS APPROACH. Press Start 2P draws T as a top bar
  over a centred stem, so it carries empty space on both sides at the baseline
  and drifts away from POCKET toward ERA. The only lever CSS gives you on a
  plain text node is letter-spacing -- and that moves EVERY glyph. Each pass
  that pulled the T closer also crushed P O C K E together, and each pass that
  relaxed the other letters let the T drift again. There is no value that fixes
  one without breaking the other, which is why five attempts traded the same
  two problems back and forth.

  WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD. The mark's text is rewritten as three parts:

      POCKE  <span class="pg-wm-t">T</span>  <span class="pg-wm-2">ERA</span>

  so the T and the second word each get their own margin. The T is pulled left
  by its own negative margin -- nothing else moves -- and ERA carries the word
  break as an explicit margin rather than a space character. letter-spacing
  goes back to 0 and word-spacing back to normal, so every other letter pair
  sits at the face's natural rhythm.

  Tuning, and these are now independent:
    --pg-wm-t   how far the T is pulled toward POCKE (more negative = closer)
    --pg-wm-gap the break before ERA (larger = wider)

  OWNERSHIP. pe-logo still owns the header mark's face, size, stretch and glow,
  and pg-footer the footer mark's. This file owns only the kerning, and it
  overrides the letter-spacing those two set by out-specifying them -- pe-logo
  doubles its class and pg-footer uses a single .nc-foot-word, so tripling and
  doubling respectively wins wherever they load.

  The split is re-applied on an interval because pg-mobile re-injects the
  header wordmark's text on its own schedule, which would otherwise wipe the
  spans.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  html body a.pg-wordmark.pg-wordmark.pg-wordmark,
  html body .nc-foot-word.nc-foot-word{
    /* the blunt instruments are off; the spans below do the work */
    letter-spacing:0 !important;
    word-spacing:normal !important;
  }

  html body .pg-wm-t{
    margin-left:-0.19em !important;
    letter-spacing:0 !important;
  }
  html body .pg-wm-2{
    margin-left:0.34em !important;
    letter-spacing:0 !important;
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgWordmark) return; window.pgWordmark = 1;

  var SEL = 'a.pg-wordmark, .nc-foot-word';

  function esc(s){
    return String(s).replace(/[&<>"]/g, function(c){
      return {'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;'}[c];
    });
  }

  /* "POCKET ERA" -> POCKE + <T> + <ERA>. Split off the LAST character of the
     first word rather than hard-coding a T, so the mark still kerns correctly
     if the brand text is ever edited in the theme settings. */
  function kern(el){
    if (el.querySelector('.pg-wm-t')) return;
    var text = (el.textContent || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
    var m = text.match(/^(\S+) (\S+)$/);
    if (!m) return;
    var first = m[1], second = m[2];
    if (first.length < 2) return;
    el.innerHTML =
      esc(first.slice(0, -1)) +
      '<span class="pg-wm-t">' + esc(first.slice(-1)) + '</span>' +
      '<span class="pg-wm-2">' + esc(second) + '</span>';
  }

  function pass(){
    document.querySelectorAll(SEL).forEach(kern);
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', pass); else pass();
  [200, 600, 1200, 2400].forEach(function(t){ setTimeout(pass, t); });
  setInterval(pass, 900);
})();
</script>
{% schema %}
{"name": "PG Wordmark", "settings": []}
{% endschema %}
